                 let 6.4 acres of the courthouse parking lot to a private
                 developer on a 75-year ground lease for office, hotel, retail
                 and residential use, and directed the County Manager to sign
                 it; he refused, believing the County's authority doubtful, and
                 the Board sought a writ of mandamus to compel its own Manager.
                 Holds, 229 Va. at 345-46, that the parcel is not "unused
                 lands" within Code sec. 15.1-261.1 because it "has a
                 substantive, functional use" as parking, and at 346-47 that
                 sec. 15.1-262's power to "sell ... or exchange and convey"
                 carries no power to lease: "Nowhere in the statute is the
                 power given to counties to grant less than fee simple title to
                 county land", and the general power to lease that sec.
                 15.1-847 gives municipalities "demonstrates a clear
                 legislative intent to withhold from counties any power to
                 lease not otherwise specifically granted." Writ denied; the
                 General Assembly granted the authority afterwards. Applies
                 Dillon's Rule expressly at 344, citing
                 commonwealthvarlington1977. With that case and
                 arlingtonvwhite2000 it is the third of the three Arlington
                 decisions the County Attorney's report lists, and the only one
                 about the County Manager Plan itself: the Board could not
                 compel its own Manager to act. Filed in sources as "legal/cases/Supreme Court of Virginia 1985 - County Board of Arlington County v. Brown.pdf", the Harvard Caselaw Access Project scan
                 of 229 Va. 341 as CourtListener serves it, saved 30 September
                 2026},
}

@jurisdiction{richmondcouncilvwilder2007,
  title       = {City of Richmond City Council v. Wilder},
  date        = {2007-11-29},
  url         = {https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8685922/city-of-richmond-city-council-v-wilder/},
  urldate     = {2026-10-01},
  journaltitle = {Va. Cir.},
  volume      = {74},
  pages       = {382},
  location    = {Richmond City Cir. Ct.},
  sortname    = {{City of Richmond City Council v. Wilder}},
  annotation  = {Spencer, J. Same docket (No. CL07-2426-4) as a July 2007
                 ruling on a demurrer, reported separately at 73 Va. Cir.
                 471, 2007 Va. Cir. LEXIS 136; this is the later ruling, on
                 cross-motions for summary judgment, where the holding sits.
                 The Mayor had declared Council's own legislative-staff
                 appointments (Council Liaisons, its Chief of Staff, Policy
                 Analyst, Public Relations Specialist and Budget Analyst,
                 the Director of Legislative Services, and the City
                 Assessor) void, and the Deputy Chief Administrative Officer
                 had told those employees to reapply for their jobs.
                 Richmond is a mayor-council city with an appointed Chief
                 Administrative Officer, not a County Manager Plan locality,
                 and its Charter sec. 5.03 carries the non-interference
                 language this report's search was for: "the mayor, council,
                 and its members shall deal with the administrative services
                 solely through the chief administrative officer, and
                 neither the mayor, council, nor any member thereof shall
                 give orders ... to any subordinate of the chief
                 administrative officer." Holds that sec. 5.03's "ultimate
                 responsibility" sentence gives the CAO authority over
                 administrative and executive branch employees only, read
                 together with secs. 5.02 and 5.04 and with the
                 separation-of-powers clause of the Virginia Constitution's
                 Art. III sec. 1, so Council keeps the implied power under
                 its Charter sec. 4.02 to appoint the legislative staff it
                 needs to do its own legislative work; cites the Dillon's
                 Rule holding of \textit{Commonwealth v. Arlington County
                 Bd.}, 217 Va. 558, 574 (1977), the same case cited here as
                 commonwealthvarlington1977. Summary judgment for Council.
                 Neither opinion under this docket cites \textit{County
                 Board of Arlington County v. Brown}, and CourtListener,
                 Justia and a general web search turn up no appeal of either
                 ruling to the Court of Appeals of Virginia or the Supreme
                 Court of Virginia. Filed in sources as
                 "legal/cases/Richmond City Circuit Court 2007 - City of Richmond City Council v. Wilder.pdf", the CourtListener
                 scan read in full
                 in the browser (the automated fetch was answered with a
                 Cloudflare challenge), saved 1 October 2026},
}

