                 2026; the site refuses an automated request and its page
                 images are behind a login, so no copy of the image was had,
                 and the OCR is poor enough in places that it is quoted here as
                 it stands.},
  sortname    = {{Daily Sun}},
}

@article{dailysun1952results,
  title       = {City Charter for Arlington Soundly Beaten},
  journaltitle = {Daily Sun},
  location    = {Arlington, Va.},
  date        = {1952-11-05},
  pages       = {1},
  url         = {https://virginiachronicle.com/?a=d&d=TDS19521105.1.1},
  urldate     = {2026-10-01},
  annotation  = {The result. "With a record 36,673 voters casting their ballots
                 in the general elections in the county, 27,523 opposed
                 Incorporation, while only 3,540 favored It", following the
                 Citizens Study Commission on Local Government, appointed by
                 the County Board, which had found the county would lose about
                 $750,000 in state and federal road funds and that "there are
                 defects in the proposed Arlington City Charter, approved by
                 the 1946 Virginia General Assembly". On the rest: "While
                 turning down the incorporation proposal, the Arlington voters
                 decided to make three changes in their county government. They
                 voted to give the county manager power to appoint department
                 heads and to make his tenure indefinite. Previously the County
                 Board appointed department heads and the county manager was
                 hired by annual contract. The present contract of County
                 Manager A. T. Lundberg expires December 31." The tallies:
                 indefinite tenure 19,024 to 11,069, department heads 18,597 to
                 11,162, and a Department of Real Estate Assessments 17,795 to
                 11,348, after which "The County Board now may establish this
                 department immediately by ordinance or resolution." So
                 Arlington's strong-manager form was voted in rather than
                 legislated in, under the questions vaacts1952c198 and
                 vaacts1952c610c611 print. The page also carries the photograph
                 of the three Arlington Independent Movement winners of the
                 special elections - Alvin F. Kimel, Mrs. Leone B. Buchholz and
                 Dr. Robert H. Detwiler - "replacing three members removed from
                 office by a court ruling against federal employes serving in
                 county and state offices in Virginia" (paolicelli1952), and
                 reports that Robert W. Cox, the ousted chairman, had been
                 appointed executive officer of the Federal Communications
                 Commission, where he had been budget officer. Filed in sources
                 as "press/arlington_daily_sun/Daily Sun 1952 - City Charter For Arlington Soundly Beaten (5 November 1952, p. 1).pdf", the
                 page text as Virginia Chronicle serves it at f=XML, set out on
                 a plain page by code/sources/pages.py and printed on 1 October
                 2026; the site refuses an automated request and its page
                 images are behind a login, so no copy of the image was had,
                 and the OCR is poor enough in places that it is quoted here as
                 it stands.},
  sortname    = {{Daily Sun}},
}

